<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Supporting Our Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Does the Government Support Communities?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify articles for easier understanding. Text: Paraphrase short passages in a simple fashion.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify articles on community support, using visuals or diagrams for main points (e.g., "This diagram shows how Jóvenes Unidos por la Educación provides support to improve school facilities, increase resources, and create safer learning environments.")
            Can paraphrase short passages on volunteer work, using gestures and simple language (e.g., "Volunteering means helping without being paid, like when we clean the streets or help at food banks. I can show this by acting like I'm picking up trash and handing out food to people.")
            Can explain complex community terms using simple definitions and examples (e.g., "Volunteering is when people help others without expecting money, like organizing a park clean-up or helping at an animal shelter. It's about supporting the community.")
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a complex news headline or a short, formal paragraph (2-3 sentences) from a government report about a local community support initiative (e.g., "The Ministry of Social Development has been allocating substantial resources to underserved areas, promoting sustainable community development through collaborative projects."). Students are asked to read it quickly and silently. The teacher then asks, "How would you explain this to your younger sibling or a new neighbor who doesn't speak much English?" encouraging them to identify key terms and simplify the message.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models simplifying the displayed headline/paragraph using simpler vocabulary and passive voice. For example, for "The Ministry of Social Development has been allocating substantial resources to underserved areas," the teacher might say: "This means money and help *are being given* by the government to communities that don't have much. They *are working* to make these areas better for everyone." The teacher also demonstrates using gestures for words like 'help' or 'better'. Pronunciation focus: Teacher explicitly stresses content words like 'Ministry', 'Social', 'Development', 'allocating', 'resources', 'underserved', 'areas' and practices the rhythm of the simplified sentence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we simplify, do we change the original meaning of the text? (No, we keep the main idea.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use all the same complex words, or do we try to use easier ones? (Easier ones.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we use gestures or pictures to help explain? (Yes, always helpful!)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Supporting Our Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students are provided with a short article excerpt (approx. 150-200 words) from a local Panamanian news website about a government-supported volunteer brigade or a community development initiative (e.g., "Programa Techos de Esperanza"). The text will incorporate target vocabulary such as 'government', 'programs', 'resources', 'services', 'volunteers', 'projects', 'community', 'fund', 'support', 'allocate', 'improve', 'promote', 'contribute', 'underserved', 'impactful', 'essential', 'public services', 'development', 'involvement' and include instances of passive voice and present perfect continuous.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Explore how different communities express and value support needs through language and communication by identifying core messages in an authentic text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main purpose of the community program described in the article?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read 3-4 statements related to the article and mark them True or False (e.g., "The program is solely funded by volunteers. (False)").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match complex terms from the article (e.g., 'public services', 'sustainable development') with their simpler definitions or examples provided on a separate list.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice simplifying complex sentences, paraphrasing passages, and explaining terms using: Passive voice (e.g., "Community programs are funded by the government."), Modals for necessity (e.g., "Individuals should contribute to community projects."), Present perfect continuous (e.g., "Volunteers have been working to improve community services."). Target vocabulary: *government, programs, resources, services, responsibilities, volunteers, projects, community, influence, public services, development, impact, underserved, meaningful, positive, crucial, responsible, active, impactful, essential, support, fund, allocate, improve, promote, contribute, assess, address, participate, engage, provide, clean up.* Pronunciation focus: Alternating stressed and unstressed syllables for fluency.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Simplification:</b> The teacher provides 5-7 complex sentences extracted from the article. Students, individually, rewrite each sentence using simpler vocabulary and grammar, focusing on passive voice where appropriate. E.g., "The administration has been actively engaging citizens in the decision-making processes for local infrastructure development." can be simplified to "Citizens *are involved* by the government when deciding about new buildings in the community."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Paraphrase Relay:</b> In small groups of three, students are given a different short passage (2-3 sentences) from the article. Student A reads the passage. Student B paraphrases it in simpler terms, using gestures and target vocabulary. Student C then summarizes Student B's paraphrase even more concisely. They rotate roles with new passages. The teacher monitors for correct use of passive voice, modals, and present perfect continuous (e.g., "Volunteers *have been dedicating* their time to clean up local parks.") and provides feedback on clarity and stress patterns.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Explain a Term:</b> Students work in pairs. One student picks a complex term from a provided list (e.g., 'civic responsibilities', 'underserved communities', 'sustainable development'). Their partner has to explain it in simple language, using an example from the article or real life. They then switch roles. (e.g., "Civic responsibilities are the things we *should do* as citizens, like voting or helping our community. We *should contribute* to these projects.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Supporting Our Community."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Community Service Campaign Explanation.</b> Students, in their small groups, present a specific element of their "Community Service Campaign" project developed in previous lessons. The task for this lesson is to *mediate* information about their campaign to another group who might be unfamiliar with the project or community service. Each group chooses one of the following roles:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Role 1: Campaign Ambassador.</b> Present the main goal and a key volunteer opportunity of their campaign. They must simplify a complex aspect of how the government supports this type of work (e.g., "Local volunteer projects *are often funded* by municipal grants, meaning money *is provided* to help start or expand these efforts.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Role 2: Impact Communicator.</b> Explain the expected positive 'impact' of their campaign and paraphrase a short passage about why volunteer *involvement* is crucial. They *should* use simple definitions and examples, perhaps using a visual aid (diagram or simple drawing). (e.g., "The community's development *has been improved* by these projects, and it's essential everyone contributes.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Role 3: Policy Simplifier.</b> Take a complex policy or governmental process related to community *resources* or *services* (e.g., applying for support, reporting needs) and simplify it for the audience, perhaps using a step-by-step diagram or flow chart. (e.g., "Applications for support *are submitted* online; then, they *are reviewed* by a committee.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are encouraged to use gestures, simple language, and visuals to ensure their message is clearly understood by their peers. They will engage in discussions about civic responsibilities ("Why has community involvement been important for everyone?") and lead collaborative discussions, offering diverse solutions for community involvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their chosen "Community Service Campaign" element (as defined in Stage 4) to another group. The teacher observes using a checklist, and the listening group uses a peer assessment rubric focusing on mediation skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Simplicity:</b> Was the complex information about government support or volunteer work simplified effectively? (Did they explain complex community terms using simple definitions and examples?)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Did they use passive voice to explain support structures (e.g., "Community programs *are funded* by the government.")? Did they use modals for necessity (e.g., "Individuals *should contribute* to community projects.")?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary & Pronunciation:</b> Was target vocabulary used appropriately and pronounced intelligibly, with attention to stress patterns?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Visuals/Gestures:</b> Were visuals or gestures used effectively to aid understanding when simplifying or paraphrasing?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Did the group members work together effectively to mediate the information? (Sociolinguistic: Led collaborative discussions offering diverse solutions.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about simplifying information today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now explain __________ (a complex term) using simpler language by..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel in my ability to take a complex article about community support and simplify it for others?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategies did I find most effective for explaining new or difficult terms to my peers?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of mediation (simplifying, paraphrasing, explaining) did students grasp easily, and where did they struggle? Did the focus on passive voice and modals effectively aid simplification? Were the pronunciation exercises beneficial for rhythmic speech when mediating? What adjustments are needed for future mediation tasks, perhaps providing more examples of visuals or practicing specific types of paraphrasing? How can the "Community Service Campaign" be further integrated into future themes?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "How Does the Government Support Communities?" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of learning objectives, building on the communicative skills developed throughout this unit, particularly the ability to communicate complex ideas clearly and effectively.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>